  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "daemon-config-runtimes-grok",
      "impact": "additive",
      "summary": "DaemonConfig.runtimes.grok takes compatInstructions, agentUserMcpServers and versionPolicy, and createDaemon builds the Grok adapter from configuration when runtimeAllowlist lists grok (#1094). Without grok in the allowlist nothing changes; a Host that assembles GrokAdapter itself can move those options into DaemonConfig.",
      "migration": "docs/device-setup.md#runtime-isolation-and-tool-authorization",
      "items": [
        "config:runtimes",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#DaemonConfig"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "server-sqlite-v8",
      "impact": "breaking",
      "acceptance": "accept",
      "summary": "The embedded server's SQLite file is now schema 8 (#1113 A2b): the activity log is persisted in the database instead of process memory, so task activity and its cursors survive a restart, and the terminal-task archive removes it in its own transaction. A schema 6 or 7 file is upgraded in place when it opens, inside the open transaction, keeping its pairings, tasks, approvals and other rows; 7 to 8 adds activity_log_head and activity_log_entry empty, since no activity history was persisted before. The upgrade is one-way: 0.32.0 and earlier refuse a schema 8 file, so stop the server and back up the database file before the first open with this release. Schema 8 also closes a task's activity log and approval timeline in the same transaction as its first terminal or accepted Host cancellation (#1113 A3); the 7 to 8 upgrade rebuilds approval_timeline_tail without the idle deadline, keeping every observation, and closes already-closed tasks at their original deadline. A v8 file missing either activity table or the closure trigger is refused as SQLITE_SCHEMA_INVALID.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1113-durable-activity-log.md#sqlite-a2b",
      "items": [
        "store:server-sqlite"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "approval-timeline-closes-with-task",
      "impact": "breaking",
      "summary": "An approval timeline no longer idle-expires (#1113 A3). It closes in the same transaction as its task's first terminal or accepted Host cancellation and expires ten minutes after that instant; reads and replays never renew it. ApprovalTimelineAppendInput loses ttlMs, and ApprovalTimelineTail gains terminalAt while its expiresAt becomes optional, present only once closed. On a closed timeline ApprovalTimelineStore.append throws the new approval_timeline_settled code and resolvePending returns absent, so a request pending at the close can no longer be decided; ByokCloud refuses a late task.await_approval with task_settled_by_cloud. A timeline keeps at most 50 observations and MAX_APPROVAL_TIMELINE_BYTES (1 MiB) of serialized observations, evicting the oldest first and never the pending request; one event is at most MAX_APPROVAL_EVENT_BYTES and capacity must be 2 to 50, else coordination_input_invalid. Archive, redaction and expiry clear a closed timeline's entries and keep its row as the fence. A custom ApprovalTimelineStore must close with its task, refuse appends once closed, and retain through retainApprovalObservations or the same bounds; pendingApprovalObservation is the shared pending fold.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1113-durable-activity-log.md#task-output-closure-a3",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#ApprovalTimelineAppendInput",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#ApprovalTimelineTail",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#MAX_APPROVAL_EVENT_BYTES",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#MAX_APPROVAL_TIMELINE_BYTES",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#pendingApprovalObservation",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#retainApprovalObservations",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#validateApprovalTimelineAppend",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#validateApprovalTimelineResolve",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#PostgresApprovalTimelineStore",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane/runtime#PostgresApprovalTimelineStore"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "task-output-closes-at-terminal",
      "impact": "breaking",
      "acceptance": "accept",
      "summary": "A task's activity log and approval timeline close in the same transaction as its first terminal or accepted Host cancellation, in every built-in composition (#1113 A3). Later task.progress, task.artifact and task.await_approval are refused with task_settled_by_cloud, also on a redacted or archived task, and both expire ten minutes after the closing instant without renewal. CLOUD_ERROR_CODES gains approval_timeline_settled. The in-memory InMemoryTaskAttemptStore, InMemoryTaskCancellationStore and InMemoryDeviceDirectory close them through the attempt state, and a custom composition must close both in the same commit as its first terminal or accepted cancellation. Postgres migration 0035 adds the closing trigger on task and approval_timeline_tail.terminal_at with a constraint that only a closed row has expires_at, drops the idle deadline of every open timeline, and closes already-closed tasks at their original deadline. Deploy 0035 and the code together: each fails against the other's approval schema. Apply 0035 only through deploy/scripts/migrate, and on a large tenant in a maintenance window: it scans task and rewrites approval_timeline_tail in one transaction that blocks writes to task.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1113-durable-activity-log.md#task-output-closure-a3",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#CLOUD_ERROR_CODES",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InMemoryDeviceDirectory",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InMemoryTaskAttemptStore",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InMemoryTaskCancellationStore",
        "store:cloud-dataplane-postgres"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "task-events-wake",
      "impact": "additive",
      "summary": "A content-free task events wake (#1113 A4): TaskEventsWake hints that a task's state, activity log or approval timeline changed, naming only the tenant and task, so a reader re-reads those sources instead of polling. createInMemoryCloudStores and the embedded SQLite composition expose taskEventsWake, which every write to those stores signals after it commits; a rollback signals nothing. createPostgresTaskEventsWake listens on a session-mode DSN for the triggers migration 0036 adds on task, activity_log_head and approval_timeline_tail (additive; either deployment order is safe, and code before 0036 reads at its fallback interval), so commits on any instance, maintenance included, wake readers on every instance. A lost hint delays a reader by at most fallbackMs (DEFAULT_TASK_EVENTS_WAKE_FALLBACK_MS, 5 seconds) and a listener reconnect wakes every subscription. createInProcessTaskEventsWake covers writers in one process only. The Postgres listener options are now the shared PostgresWakeOptions, which PostgresMailboxWakeOptions names unchanged.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1113-durable-activity-log.md#task-events-wake-a4",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#DEFAULT_TASK_EVENTS_WAKE_FALLBACK_MS",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InMemoryActivityStore",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InMemoryCloudComposition",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#InProcessTaskEventsWakeOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#TaskEventsWake",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#TaskEventsWakeSubscription",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud#createInProcessTaskEventsWake",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#PostgresMailboxWakeOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#PostgresTaskEventsWake",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#PostgresTaskEventsWakeOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#PostgresWakeOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#TaskEventsWakeProbeMissedError",
        "export:@byok-sdk/cloud-dataplane#createPostgresTaskEventsWake"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "task-events-durable-cursor",
      "impact": "breaking",
      "summary": "ByokServer.tasks.events and TaskHandle.events() read the durable activity log, task state and the approval timeline instead of an in-process relay buffer (#1113). Cursors resume across a restart over the same SQLite file, including inside a progress batch; cursors from 0.32.0 and earlier are refused as TaskEventsUnavailableError cursor_unknown. State is the current fact, coalesced, not a replay of every transition. A terminal task stays readable while its activity is retained, so the feed_expired reason is gone; events_truncated now marks activity the log dropped before the reader reached it, also for a cursor taken before the task had any activity. Readers wait on the stores' task events wake, with its five-second fallback. CreateByokServerOptions.taskEventBufferLimit and taskEventRetentionMs and the DEFAULT_TASK_EVENT_BUFFER_LIMIT and DEFAULT_TASK_EVENT_RETENTION_MS exports are removed. events.subscribe() announces task.state only for tasks this instance dispatched.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1113-task-events-durable-cursor.md#tasksevents-reads-the-durable-activity-log",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/server#CreateByokServerOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/server#DEFAULT_TASK_EVENT_BUFFER_LIMIT",
        "export:@byok-sdk/server#DEFAULT_TASK_EVENT_RETENTION_MS",
        "export:@byok-sdk/server#TaskEventsUnavailableError"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dsh-adapter",
      "impact": "additive",
      "summary": "Add DshAdapter, a DeepSeek Harness (dsh) runtime adapter over ACP (`dsh --profile acp`) reached by the custom harness id dsh (#1258). The Host constructs it with an SDK-owned persistent home that is not ~/.dsh and a provider, model and keyEnv selection; it resumes native sessions, attaches task MCP toolsets including result documents, declines auto and file-scope tasks before claim, and runs on darwin and linux. fakeRuntimeAdapter accepts 'dsh'. Nothing changes for a Host that does not construct it.",
      "migration": "https://github.com/Ancienttwo/byok-sdk/issues/1258",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#DshAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#DshAdapterOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#DshModelSelection",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#DshSandboxMode",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#DshAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#DshAdapterOptions",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#DshModelSelection",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#DshSandboxMode",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/testing#FakeRuntimeAdapterId"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "task-fail-quota-scope",
      "impact": "additive",
      "summary": "A quota task.fail.failure may carry scope: 'account' or 'model' (#1271), and TaskResult.failure and TerminalResult.failure copy it. The Claude adapter sets it from rate_limit_info.rateLimitType: five_hour and seven_day are account, seven_day_opus and seven_day_sonnet are model, so another model of the same runtime may still run; any other value or none gives no scope, and other runtimes never set it. A daemon sends scope only to a server that advertises the new runtime-quota-scope flag, which the reference server and hosted cloud do; to an older server it sends class and resetsAt without it. @byok-sdk/protocol adds RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPE_CAPABILITY, RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPES and the RuntimeQuotaScope type, and TaskFailureReading gains scope. A Host that reads failure.class needs no change; one that switches models on a model-scoped quota reads scope and treats it as absent when missing.",
      "migration": "docs/spec.md#post-admission-runtime-failure-authority",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#CAPABILITY_FLAGS",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPES",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPE_CAPABILITY",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#RuntimeQuotaScope",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#TaskFailPayloadSchema",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#TaskFailSealedPayload",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#TaskFailureReading",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#TaskFailureSchema",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol/constants#CAPABILITY_FLAGS",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol/constants#RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPES",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol/constants#RUNTIME_QUOTA_SCOPE_CAPABILITY"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "adapter-admission-declarations",
      "impact": "additive",
      "summary": "RuntimeAdapterDescriptor gains optional fileScope ({ lanes }) and providerProfileBinding, and RuntimeAdapter gains an optional projectMcpEnvironment(env). The daemon reads these declarations for file-scope admission, the provider-profile-binding capability and the MCP environment of probes and task MCP servers, instead of checking for the runtime id pi. PiAdapter declares all three, so built-in behavior is unchanged. A custom adapter needs no change; one that reused the id pi without enforcing a file scope no longer admits scoped offers until it declares fileScope.",
      "migration": "docs/adapter-capabilities.md#local-admission-declarations",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#PiAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#RuntimeAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client#RuntimeAdapterDescriptor",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#PiAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#RuntimeAdapter",
        "export:@byok-sdk/client/adapters#RuntimeAdapterDescriptor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sealed-payload-v2-protocol",
      "impact": "breaking",
      "summary": "The protocol defines sealed payload v2 (#1045 S1): TaskSealingSchema (and so the cloud's SealedAgentExecutionInputSchema) accepts the agent-message and memory-projection categories, gated by the new sealed-payload-v2 flag, and memory-projection needs task-output in the same policy. agent.message.publish, agent.message.disposition and the Agent-memory projection mutation and receipt are now unions of a plaintext and a sealed branch; the plaintext branch has its own name (AgentMessagePublishPlainPayload, AgentMessageDispositionPlainPayload, AgentMemoryProjectionPlainMutation, AgentMemoryProjectionPlainReceipt and their schemas), so narrow with payload.sealed === undefined or use the plaintext name. SEALED_PAYLOAD_V1_CATEGORIES and SealedPayloadV1Category are removed; use SEALED_PAYLOAD_CATEGORIES and SealedPayloadCategory. isSealedTaskPlainOutput takes the task's frozen sealing categories as a third argument. New: SEALED_PAYLOAD_V2_CAPABILITY, sealedMovedFields and sealedAgentMessageMaxContentLength. No package seals or admits v2 content yet.",
      "migration": "docs/migrations/1045-e2ee-v2.md#capabilities",
      "items": [
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#AgentMemoryProjectionCommitRequestSchema",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#AgentMemoryProjectionCommitResponseSchema",
        "export:@byok-sdk/protocol#AgentMemoryProjectionMutationSchema",
